# What the build context must not carry.
#
# The backend image copies the application in, so everything here is either a
# secret that must never be baked into a layer, an artefact the build produces
# for itself, or weight that has no business in the context at all.
#
# Note that docker/ is deliberately absent: both Dockerfiles read from it.

# ─── Secrets ───
# The real environment file lives beside the example on a developer machine and
# in CI. It is git-ignored, but the build context is the filesystem, not the
# index, so without this line every credential in it ships inside the image.
.env
backend/.env
backend/.env.*
!backend/.env.example

# ─── Dependencies the build resolves for itself ───
# vendor/ is installed from composer.lock inside the build. Copying the host's
# would make the image depend on whatever that machine happens to hold.
**/vendor/
**/node_modules/

# ─── Version control and CI ───
.git/
.gitattributes
.gitignore
.github/

# ─── Repository material with no role at runtime ───
docs/
scripts/
Makefile
README.md
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.*.yml

# ─── Generated state, not source ───
backend/storage/logs/*
backend/storage/framework/cache/*
backend/storage/framework/sessions/*
backend/storage/framework/testing/*
backend/storage/framework/views/*
backend/storage/pail/
backend/bootstrap/cache/*
backend/.phpunit.cache/
backend/.phpunit.result.cache
backend/public/hot
backend/public/storage
